#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d6988b-3220-578f-ba1c-cd86b9e3dbf9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and the Radius"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89462485-2e28-5727-82c7-7afd0cac6d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle Given Its Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47863922-b0d5-5a0b-8a01-ffefe3b3c929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Endpoints of a Diameter"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3520d323-c6a4-5bce-bac1-1f4c02bd0068" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and a Point on the Circle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088874 :tutorial/content "What equation represents a circle of radius $5$ centered at $C$ with coordinates $(1,3)?$\n\nLet's refer to the following figure.\n\n![](images/7a/7adbae4fd1b881931f683d6f4245cd8ee6dfae46fdffcf1b5834448be645d4b5.png)\n\nLet's take a generic point $A$ with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the circumference. Now, we can use the distance formula to write down the length of the radius $CA\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nCA = \\sqrt{(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we know that the radius of the circle is $CA=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(x-1)^2 + (y-3)^2}  = 5.\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides gives us the **equation of the circle** of radius $5$ centered at $C(1,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 + (y-3)^2 = 25.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a circle of radius $r$ centered at point $C$ with coordinates $(a,b),$ the general equation will be\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "145c60c7-47b9-5181-b777-79ad3d8e3704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2439 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
